Now I understand the flow. The crash is an "Invalid-free" in `frame_get_lazychunk` -> `frame_decompress_chunk` -> `blosc2_schunk_decompress_chunk`. 

Looking at `frame_get_lazychunk`: when `frame->cframe == NULL` (disk-based frame), it allocates `*chunk = malloc(lazychunk_cbytes)` and sets `*needs_free = true`. But if `rc < 0` at the `end:` label, it does `free(*chunk)` and sets `*chunk = NULL`. 

Wait, but the crash is an invalid free. Let me look more carefully. In `frame_get_lazychunk`, when `frame->cframe != NULL` (in-memory frame), `*chunk = frame->cframe + header_len + offset` and `*needs_free` stays `false`. Then in `frame_decompress_chunk`, if `needs_free` is true, it frees `src`. 

The issue: when `frame->cframe == NULL` path, `*needs_free = true` is set. But if `rc < 0` at `end:`, it frees `*chunk` and sets `*chunk = NULL`, but does NOT reset `*needs_free` to false. Then `frame_decompress_chunk` would call `free(src)` where `src` is NULL... but free(NULL) is valid.

Actually wait - let me re-read. The issue is the `end:` block:
```c
end:
  if (fp != NULL) {
    fclose(fp);
  }
  if (rc < 0) {
    if (needs_free) {
      free(*chunk);
      *chunk = NULL;
    }
    return rc;
  }
```

Hmm, `if (needs_free)` - `needs_free` is a `bool*` pointer, always non-NULL. So it frees `*chunk` and sets it to NULL. But `*needs_free` remains `true`. Then in `frame_decompress_chunk`:
```c
  rc = frame_get_lazychunk(frame, nchunk, &src, &needs_free);
  if (rc < 0) {
    ...
    return rc;
  }
```
If rc < 0, it returns early, so no double free there.

Let me look at the actual bug more carefully. The crash state is `frame_get_lazychunk`, `frame_decompress_chunk`, `blosc2_schunk_decompress_chunk`. Let me check the git log to understand what changed around 2021-03-01.
